config TLSR_BOOT_GUARD_FEEDER_STACK_SIZE
	int "The watchdog feeder thread's stack, in octets"
	default 512
	depends on TLSR_BOOT_GUARD
	help
	  The thread that feeds the watchdog every quarter period
	  (tlsr_boot_guard.c): 184 octets deep at most in the emulator.

config TLSR_BOOT_GUARD_FEEDER_PRIO_ABOVE_LOWEST
	int "The watchdog feeder's priority: this many levels above the lowest application priority"
	default 0
	range 0 4
	depends on TLSR_BOOT_GUARD
	help
	  The feeder (tlsr_boot_guard.c) feeds the watchdog while every thread
	  above its priority lets it run; a thread there that hangs resets the
	  chip. 0: the lowest application priority, shared with background
	  threads such as ZMK Studio's RPC thread, which then rely on time
	  slicing, and Zephyr restarts a thread's slice whenever a higher
	  priority one preempts it. 1: one level above them, so a long
	  background job (Studio encoding a large keymap) never keeps the
	  feeder from running; a hang at the lowest priority is then not
	  caught.

config ZMK_KEYMAP_SMALL_POSITION_TABLES
	bool "ZMK's per-position keymap tables in one octet a position"
	help
	  ZMK keeps, for every key position, the layer state at its press
	  (app/src/keymap.c) and, with physical layouts, the position's place
	  in the stock layout (app/src/physical_layouts.c), each in 32 bits.
	  With this, each takes one octet: for keymaps of at most eight layers
	  and fewer than 255 positions (asserted at build). Three octets of
	  RAM a position for each table.

