config TLSR_USB_WIRED_POSITION_ONLY
	bool "USB only in the wired position of the mode switch"
	depends on ZMK_USB && !TLSR_BLE_IGNORE_MODE_SWITCH
	select ZMK_USB_ENABLE_ON_REQUEST
	help
	  src/ble/ble.c enables USB (zmk_usb_enable()) once the mode switch
	  reads wired at boot, and never in the BT or 2.4G positions: the
	  cable there only brings power, and the keyboard does not attach to
	  the bus. With TLSR_USB_POLL_TIMER its poll starts then too.

config TLSR_USB_ON_REQUEST
	bool "A key brings USB up in the BT and 2.4G positions (&usb_on)"
	default y
	depends on TLSR_USB_WIRED_POSITION_ONLY && DT_HAS_ZMK_BEHAVIOR_USB_REQUEST_ENABLED
	help
	  src/behavior_usb_request.c: the &usb_on key (zmk,behavior-usb-request)
	  enables USB, with its poll, in a position of the mode switch that
	  has none at boot, until the power goes or the switch moves. It is
	  for the host tools there (the update receiver's information, the
	  links' counters, ZMK Studio): HID reports go over USB only when the
	  switch read wired at boot (zmk_usb_reports_allowed()), so the keys
	  stay on the radio link. With a host on the cable the keyboard does
	  not go to deep sleep, and USB and its poll take CPU from the link,
	  so it is not left on. In the wired position the key does nothing.

config TLSR_BLE_MODE_FROM_POWER_IN
	bool "The mode from the cable's power and a link chosen by key, on a board without a mode switch"
	depends on BATTERY_ADC && !TLSR_BLE_IGNORE_MODE_SWITCH
	help
	  A board whose power switch only turns the battery on (the V65 V3):
	  the mode comes from the cable's power (the battery node's power-in
	  pin) and the link chosen by key (&link_mode, kept with the BLE
	  profiles): with power in, USB or the chosen wireless link; without
	  it, the wireless link (USB falls back to BLE; choosing USB without the
	  cable is ignored). Read at boot as a mode
	  switch is and watched the same way: a change of either reboots the
	  chip into the new mode.

config TLSR_BLE_SWITCH_POLL_MS
	int "Period of the mode switch watch with power in (ms)"
	default 50
	range 20 500
	help
	  src/ble/ble.c reads the mode switch this often while power is in
	  (every second on the battery), and reboots into the position it
	  moved to. Each read turns the switch pins' pull-ups on (and drives
	  the switch's common pin, where a board has one) and runs code that,
	  on a board with a small instruction cache, misses it: a longer
	  period costs less CPU and only delays the reboot.

config TLSR_BLE_TX_POWER_DEFAULT
	hex "TX power level when the per-unit byte 0x76009 is blank"
	default 0x2b
	help
	  Bit 7 goes to radio register 0x1225 bit 6, bits 5:0 to 0x1226 bit 7
	  and 0x1227 bits 4:0 (tlsr_radio_set_power_level(); not documented).

